from build import put,render
common='Official repository located and pinned; model, training, inference and evaluation source inspection is still in progress. No benchmark code executed.'
def b(pid,desc,cond,cause,evidence,follow,status='Partially addressed',kind='Methodological',conf='High within the evaluated setting'):
 return dict(id=pid,description=desc,conditions=cond,cause=cause,evidence=evidence,kind=kind,followups=follow,status=status,confidence=conf)
put('knowno',title='Robots That Ask For Help: Uncertainty Alignment for Large Language Model Planners',authors=['Allen Z. Ren','Anushri Dixit','Alexandra Bodrova','Sumeet Singh','Stephen Tu','Noah Brown','Peng Xu','Leila Takayama','Fei Xia','Jake Varley','Zhenjia Xu','Dorsa Sadigh','Andy Zeng','Anirudha Majumdar'],year=2023,venue='CoRL 2023; PMLR volume 229',pages=['3–8','12–16'],problem='An LLM planner must choose actions under ambiguous language and request human clarification only when necessary. This is manipulation/planning research rather than an R2R route-following method. Context includes already grounded textual observations; the theorem assumes correct low-level execution and accurate human help.',method='An LLM proposes four candidate actions and an appended other-action option. A second prompt scores the answer letters. Calibration uses nonconformity 1 minus the true-label score and a finite-sample conformal quantile. At inference, labels above the threshold form a prediction set; a singleton is executed, otherwise a human selects a valid option or halts. For a fixed calibration dataset the paper uses a beta-quantile tolerance bound with N=400 and delta=0.01. Multi-step calibration uses the minimum true-label score across an entire correct-action sequence, then constructs per-step sets causally with the same threshold. Appendix A3 handles several acceptable actions by following the highest-scored acceptable branch during both calibration and human-assisted deployment. No model fine-tuning is required; labelled calibration scenarios and human intervention are required. Most experiments use PaLM-2L; ensemble baselines require 20 queries.',novelty='Extends conformal prediction to language-planning choices and dependent multi-step execution, contrasting prompt-only uncertainty and cumulative-softmax thresholding. Coverage does not require accurate model probabilities; minimal expected set-size claims require the additional assumption that the relevant score models true conditional probabilities. Minimal set size is a different objective from minimizing the probability of requesting help or human response effort.',results='PAPER EXPERIMENT: Hardware Table 1, p. 7, KnowNo plan success 0.76, task success 0.74, per-step help 0.58 versus Simple Set 0.76/0.72/0.72 in 50 trials. Hardware mobile manipulation Table 2, p. 8, plan success 0.87 and physical task success 0.76 with help 0.67; matched-plan-success Simple Set needs help 0.81. The planning versus physical success gap directly demonstrates the limit of the execution assumption. Appendix A4 has only ten bimanual trials. DEDUCTION: the coverage guarantee is distributional, not a guarantee conditional on each particular task or on only the no-help subset. Sequence-level minimum scores are not generally joint sequence probabilities, so do not infer optimal help rate from calibrated step scores. The paper’s own §6 acknowledges grounding/control uncertainty and inaccurate human responses are outside scope.',implementation='The official project links google-research/google-research/language_model_uncertainty, which contains three notebooks and a README. Notebook source is being retrieved and audited without execution; the release should not be confused with a complete robot control stack.',bottlenecks=[b('knowno-grounding','Planning coverage does not cover perception errors, failed low-level control, or a changed scenario distribution.','Ambiguous or incorrect textual grounding, physical execution failures, or deployment shifts.','Calibration is over grounded planning choices from an exchangeable scenario distribution; correct human help is assumed.','AUTHOR ACKNOWLEDGEMENT: §6; PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 2 plan 0.87 versus task 0.76.',['Explore until Confident','AIUTA'],'Still unresolved in the reviewed literature','Assumption and insufficiently tested')],sources=[dict(url='https://proceedings.mlr.press/v229/ren23a.html',locator='Official proceedings'),dict(url='https://robot-help.github.io/',locator='Official project and repository link')],limits=['Remaining appendix scenario/prompt pages and notebook audit pending. No claim of physical deployment reproduction.'])
put('explore_confident',title='Explore until Confident: Efficient Exploration for Embodied Question Answering',authors=['Allen Z. Ren','Jaden Clark','Anushri Dixit','Masha Itkina','Anirudha Majumdar','Dorsa Sadigh'],year=2024,venue='RSS 2024',pages=['3–12'],problem='Explore an unknown indoor scene until there is sufficient visual evidence to answer a multiple-choice question. Inputs include RGB-D, camera intrinsics, localization, and a collision-free local planner. Simulation assumes perfect odometry and movements of at most 3 m between queried viewpoints.',method='Prismatic VLM scores answer options, image-question relevance, and exploration directions marked on the RGB image. Local semantic value normalizes scores among three projected free-space points; global semantic value scores whether any direction is worth exploring. Their exponentially scaled sum is smoothed into a TSDF-derived map and weights frontier sampling. The map is built online from depth. For stopping, rho_t(y)=Rel_t*(p_t(y)-1), and episode score is min_t rho_t(y). A conformal threshold calibrated on full exploration episodes gives per-step answer sets whose running intersection retains the correct answer simultaneously over all prefixes with marginal probability at least 1-epsilon under the same scenario distribution and policy. Stop when the intersection is a singleton. At timeout or an empty intersection, choose the most likely answer at the most relevant view: this fallback is outside the singleton guarantee. Low relevance gives scores near zero, intentionally preventing irrelevant views from removing many answers. The main method has no additional VLM training but uses 300 labelled calibration episodes; an optional separate fine-tuning experiment is reported.',novelty='Combines visually prompted semantic frontier exploration with sequence-calibrated answer stopping. KnowNo already supplies multi-step conformal calibration; here the set is intersected over repeated observations of a common answer rather than used to request human action labels. This is not a general proof of optimal exploration or conditional accuracy on stopped episodes.',results='HM-EQA contains 500 GPT-4V-generated, human-filtered questions across 267 HM3D scenes. Exploration comparisons use all 500 at fixed horizons; stopping comparisons split 300 calibration/200 test. Figure 9, p. 10: about 58% answer success requires 71% of the maximum steps versus 85% for a relevance-threshold baseline. Main raw VLM success saturates near 60%; §VII-E reports a separate fine-tuned model 56.2→68.1%. Six hardware scenarios yield four correct answers for this method and relevance baseline; several relevant views still produce wrong answers. These results support efficient exploration and calibrated set retention, not universal high answer accuracy. §VIII acknowledges orientation is omitted from semantic values, single-view reasoning is limited, and prediction sets do not yet guide exploration.',implementation=common,bottlenecks=[b('explore-answer','A useful view can still produce a confident incorrect answer, and forced fallback answers lack the conformal retention guarantee.','VLM perception/spatial reasoning errors; timeout or empty-set fallback; distribution or exploration-policy change.','The decision mechanism calibrates scores but does not improve the underlying visual reasoning; fallback returns a point answer without retained-set coverage.','PAPER EXPERIMENT: §VII-C/H and Figure 10; METHOD: §V-B.',['AIUTA','VSI-Bench','OmniAgent'],'Partially addressed')],sources=[dict(url='https://explore-eqa.github.io/',locator='Official project'),dict(url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.15941',locator='Author manuscript'),dict(url='https://github.com/Stanford-ILIAD/explore-eqa',locator='Official code')],limits=['The supplied 14-page PDF cites an appendix that is not embedded; alternate complete version/supplement search pending. No robot experiment executed.'])
put('gsavln',title='General Scene Adaptation for Vision-and-Language Navigation',authors=['Haodong Hong','Yanyuan Qiao','Sen Wang','Jiajun Liu','Qi Wu'],year=2025,venue='ICLR 2025',pages=['3–10','17–28'],problem='Repeated instructions in a persistent scene permit memory-based adaptation beyond independent-episode VLN. GSA-R2R extends evaluation to 150 residential/non-residential MP3D/HM3D scenes and synthetic Basic, Scene, and User styles. It evaluates within-scene accumulation, not necessarily retention across a continual stream of different domains.',method='A speaker generates route instructions, a navigation model identifies failures, GPT-4 refines failed instructions from panoramas/path visualizations, and GPT-4o rewrites styles using building-user roles or character profiles. Six hundred paths per scene are sampled. GR-DUET retains one topological graph across episodes, including node positions and visual observations, while resetting per-episode visited flags. Offline pretraining exposes complete training-scene graphs; fine-tuning accumulates scene-specific graphs and resets them after 50 episodes. PREVALENT augmentation improves path diversity. At test time the proposed method adapts its graph inputs rather than necessarily updating weights. Evaluation uses batch 1, three shuffled instruction sequences, and checkpoint selection by mean validation SPL. The paper reports 180 million parameters, up to 4.3 GB GPU memory, and 67 ms inference excluding feature extraction; those are author measurements on their setup.',novelty='The benchmark broadens persistent-scene adaptation to environmental and language-style shifts. GR-DUET preserves graph structure unlike TourHAMT’s long history-token stream and trains for the resulting expanded input. It is a DUET derivative and should be separated from supervised continual learning and feedback-based online parameter updates.',results='Table 4: residential Basic DUET SR/SPL 57.7/47.0→69.3/64.3; non-residential 48.1/37.3→56.6/51.5. Table 6 Scene 39.6/30.1→48.1/42.8. Table 7 shows joint dependence on full-graph pretraining plus PREVALENT: augmentation alone hurts, and neither component alone attains the combined result. ScaleVLN’s HM3D training overlaps GSA scenes and some Basic instructions, explicitly flagged as leakage in §4.3.1. The dataset-generation speaker also includes evaluation splits (§3.3.2 footnote); this concerns dataset construction and must not be conflated with downstream agent training. Human study checks only 20 instructions with 15 participants, finding roughly 77–83% path matching; styled data are not certified semantics-preserving. Appendix A6 already tests LLM rewriting: SR 42.30→44.83 versus Basic 46.37. Its label Val-R-Scene conflicts with the stated absence of residential Scene splits. Appendix A7 reports only 94/150 scenes with positive adaptation slope and weaker adaptation in heterogeneous multi-floor layouts. Thus graph reuse helps, but language adaptation and evaluation noise remain.',implementation=common,bottlenecks=[b('gsa-style','Persistent visual graph reuse does not explicitly learn an individual’s instruction style.','Repeated User-style paraphrases, unusual vocabulary, or instructions whose synthetic refinement changed route meaning.','Graph nodes store visual observations; there is no language-specific adaptation mechanism, and generated instructions retain errors.','AUTHOR ACKNOWLEDGEMENT: Appendix A11; PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 16 incomplete rewriting recovery and Table 2 human path matching.',['slow4fast-VLN','VLN-Trans'],'Partially addressed'),b('gsa-map','Map accumulation is less effective when prior observations come from structurally different floors.','Large multi-floor scenes with heterogeneous layouts.','Stored visual/topological context may be irrelevant to the current route; graph coverage alone does not ensure useful retrieval.','PAPER EXPERIMENT/author interpretation: Appendix A7, Tables 17–19.',['M3E'],'Partially addressed')],sources=[dict(url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.17403',locator='Author manuscript'),dict(url='https://github.com/honghd16/GSA-VLN',locator='Official ICLR release')],associated=['Embedded appendices A3–A11, including prompts and runtime tables'],limits=['Appendix A1/A2 and source review pending. t-SNE separation is descriptive, not proof of distributional independence.'])
put('m3e',title='M³E: Continual Vision-and-Language Navigation via Mixture of Macro and Micro Experts',authors=['Yongliang Jiang','Huaidong Zhang','Xuandi Luo','Shengfeng He'],year=2026,venue='ICLR 2026',pages=['3–10','15–19'],problem='Replay-free adaptation to sequential scene domains while retaining previous navigation skill. The protocol trains on 80% of each selected val-unseen scene and evaluates held-out episodes and previous domains; this is supervised domain-incremental learning, not unlabeled online TTA.',method='NaviLLM/Vicuna-based policy encodes 36-view panoramas and instruction/history into a decoder-only LLM. An online cognitive map supplies node features X in R^(N×d); a GNN propagates topology and instruction attention pools a scene vector. A macro MLP routes from this scene vector, while a micro MLP routes each token. Fused expert weights are beta*w_macro+(1-beta)*w_micro with beta=0.3. Six MoE-LoRA experts, top 2 active, rank 16 and scale 32 modify FFN and attention projections. After supervised learning on each new domain, summed routing weights estimate expert workload. Top-K experts receive momentum 0.4 on old parameters; other experts receive 0.6. Consolidation interpolates old and new weights. This statistic measures frequency of use, not a causal contribution to retained task performance. Batch 4, AdamW lr 2e-5, five epochs per domain, A800 80 GB GPUs; no earlier trajectories are replayed.',novelty='Hierarchical scene/token routing plus expert-dependent consolidation targets continual adaptation on an LLM agent. SAME already uses multimodal state-aware expert routing for joint multi-task training; PerpR/ESR address forgetting with stored trajectories. M3E differs in supervised sequential consolidation without replay, not in inventing MoE or scene memory.',results='Table 1 R2R AvgSR/AvgSPL 71.92/66.96 versus Dual-SR 70.25/65.40 and finetuning 63.28/59.08. Reported BWT is 0.04 on R2R but -5.91 on REVERIE (Table 2), so forgetting is not eliminated generally. Table 4 shows dual routing without momentum has BWT -6.05 despite improved SR; consolidation supplies the retention benefit. Appendix Table 7 random 30% edge dropping loses 1.09 SR, but this does not test biased false edges, localization drift, or map aliasing. Table 8 has diminishing returns from six to eight experts only on this eight-domain experiment. Eq. 14 prints FWT as R_(i-1,i)-R_(i,i), which compares pretraining versus posttraining on that same task rather than a fixed untrained baseline; its claimed interpretation as standard forward transfer is therefore unresolved until evaluator code is checked. Appendix A5 compares TTA methods with different supervision access, so its ranking does not isolate architecture alone. Nondecreasing cumulative average SPL does not logically prove each old domain is preserved.',implementation=common,bottlenecks=[b('m3e-retention','Replay-free consolidation still forgets in object-grounded navigation and uses expert workload as a retention proxy.','REVERIE domain changes or new tasks that reuse experts important for old tasks.','Frequent routing on current data does not reveal old-domain parameter importance; fixed expert capacity and interpolation do not guarantee isolation.','PAPER EXPERIMENT: Table 2 BWT -5.91; DEDUCTION from Eqs. 9–13.',['SAME','Continual VLN'],'Partially addressed')],sources=[dict(url='https://yongliangjiang.top/data/m3e/M3E_ICLR2026.pdf',locator='Author full paper'),dict(url='https://github.com/YongliangJiang/M3E-Code',locator='Official release')],limits=['Appendix A1 and implementation/metric inspection pending. No claim of formal no-forgetting or open-ended scalability.'])
put('continualvln',title='Continual Vision-and-Language Navigation',authors=['Seong Jun Jeong','Gi-Cheon Kang','Seongho Choi','Joochan Kim','Byoung-Tak Zhang'],year=2025,venue='BMVC 2025',pages=['3–9'],problem='Sequential scene-domain training causes forgetting. I-CVLN combines English R2R/RxR into 20 domains with over 600 training episodes each; D-CVLN groups CVDN scenes into 11 domains. Evaluation episodes come from the same domains with novel instructions. Domain identity is supplied during training, not inference.',method='Perplexity Replay (PerpR) ranks completed episodes by exp(-mean log probability of agent actions), retains difficult high-perplexity trajectories and removes easy ones from a fixed buffer. Training sums supervised imitation losses on current and replay data. Episodic Self-Replay (ESR) stores previous action-logit sequences and reconstructs the corresponding trajectory with stored decisions, then adds squared logit distillation to current-domain and supervised replay losses; both replay coefficients are 0.2. PerpR and ESR are separate variants, with ESR using random reservoir-style episode selection. I-CVLN uses OSCAR-initialized recurrent VLN-BERT and 500 buffered episodes; D-CVLN uses randomly initialized HAMT and 100 buffered episodes. Three scene-order curricula are averaged. No human clarification policy is learned by D-CVLN: its inputs are recorded dialogue histories.',novelty='Adds explicit continual VLN benchmarks and adapts difficulty-based rehearsal and sequential logit distillation to navigation. It differs from IVLN’s within-environment episodic memory: this is parameter learning over scene domains with retained training examples.',results='Table 1: I-CVLN vanilla AvgSR/SPL 25.6/23.1, random replay 28.0/24.9, PerpR 28.9/26.1, ESR 31.9/28.2, joint training 42.6/40.1. D-CVLN PerpR GP 6.1 versus ESR 5.8 and joint 8.1. Results establish partial forgetting mitigation with a substantial joint-training gap. Table 3 reversing PerpR selection lowers I-CVLN SR 28.9→27.1. Table 4 removing logit replay lowers SR 31.1→24.9 in a single curriculum. Table 2 does not establish strictly monotonic buffer gains: D-CVLN PerpR GP falls 6.2→6.0 from 100 to 200 episodes. Calling vanilla/joint mathematical lower/upper bounds is too strong; they are empirical comparators, and some baselines score below vanilla.',implementation='Official repository and separate supplement discovery pending. Main paper reviewed; no implementation behavior or reproduction result is asserted.',bottlenecks=[b('continual-replay','Rehearsal improves retention but remains far below joint training and requires stored previous episodes.','Many scene domains, constrained storage, or domains whose rare skills are absent from the buffer.','Finite buffer selection and sequential distillation preserve only stored behavior; confident errors may receive low perplexity and be underrepresented.','PAPER EXPERIMENT: Tables 1–4; DEDUCTION for confident-error selection.',['M3E'],'Partially addressed')],sources=[dict(url='https://bmva-archive.org.uk/bmvc/2025/assets/papers/Paper_84/paper.pdf',locator='Official BMVC paper')],limits=['Separate supplement is referenced but not embedded in supplied main PDF. Repository search and supplement audit pending.'])
put('same',title='SAME: Learning Generic Language-Guided Visual Navigation with State-Adaptive Mixture of Experts',authors=['Gengze Zhou','Yicong Hong','Zun Wang','Chongyang Zhao','Mohit Bansal','Qi Wu'],year=2025,venue='ICCV 2025',pages=['3–8'],problem='Naively mixing fine-grained route following, coarse object referring, dialogue and ObjectNav data produces conflicting navigation behavior. SAME aims for one trained policy across tasks, not continual parameter adaptation at inference.',method='DUET’s global and local cross-modal encoders are retained. Visual-query projections become sparse MoE layers with eight experts and top-2 routing. Routing input concatenates mean panoramic visual features with language CLS, projects to hidden dimension, and produces expert weights. The mixture output is a weighted sum of selected projections. DAgger supervised action loss is combined with load-balancing loss weighted 0.8. Initialization is ScaleVLN pretraining (4.9 million synthetic examples); fine-tuning samples ScaleVLN:R2R:RxR-EN:CVDN:REVERIE:SOON:Habitat-Web at 10:1:1:1:1:1:2, keeping each batch from one dataset. AdamW lr 1e-5, 20,000 iterations, batch 16, one A100 80 GB. Continuous ObjectNav demonstrations are converted to graph trajectories with endpoint and connectivity filtering; 58,803 remain. Inference uses state-conditioned experts without task identity. Zero-shot CE transfer adds a waypoint predictor, not zero-training end-to-end navigation.',novelty='Targets state-varying rather than token-only, task-only, or language-only routing and locates experts in visual query projections. It is a DUET-based multi-dataset method, distinct from VLM policy backbones and from M3E’s sequential expert consolidation.',results='Table 2 compares routing under shared initialization/data: SAME REVERIE SR 43.84 versus no MoE 40.81 and token MoE 42.02; checkpoint variation is reported across five converged checkpoints, not five independent seeds. Removing ScaleVLN initialization reduces R2R SR 73.86→59.89, much larger than the routing gain. Table 4 multi-task SAME R2R SR/SPL 76/66 versus shared-data ScaleVLN baseline 76/67; ObjectNav SR improves 72.3→76.3 but SPL falls 43.4→42.7. Thus it does not improve every metric. Table 5 CE R2R 47 SR/38 SPL trails specialized BEVBert 59/50. Table 6 latency increases DUET 28.78→59.14 ms despite only about 5.2% more FLOPs; parameter/FLOP savings are not equal to latency savings. Table 7 shows batch construction and DAgger effects much larger than small routing differences.',implementation=common,bottlenecks=[b('same-conflict','Task mixing and expert routing still trade off path efficiency and continuous-environment transfer.','Joint fine-grained and goal-only training, or graph-to-continuous transfer.','Different tasks reward distinct behavior; sparse routing does not remove waypoint/control discrepancies or all gradient conflict.','PAPER EXPERIMENT: Tables 4, 5 and 7.',['M3E','VLN-MME','Rethinking the Embodied Gap'],'Partially addressed')],sources=[dict(url='https://github.com/GengzeZhou/SAME',locator='Official implementation and ICCV metadata')],limits=['Separate supplement and code audit pending. Reported checkpoint variation is not independent-seed uncertainty.'])
put('slow4fast',title='Towards Open Environments and Instructions: General Vision-Language Navigation via Fast-Slow Interactive Reasoning',authors=['Yang Li','Aming Wu','Zihao Zhang','Yahong Han'],year=2026,venue='CVPR 2026',pages=['3–8'],problem='GSA-R2R agents must reuse experience across episodes and handle scene/user instruction styles. The fast agent is DUET; a Llama3.2-vision slow component summarizes navigation history into reusable experiences.',method='Each step logs viewpoint, topology, instruction, action, visual description and metrics. A CoT prompt turns logs into scene type, spatial context/rules, navigation strategy, historical success rate and frequency. Retrieved experiences are encoded and attended to by visual features: queries are visual features, keys/values are experience vectors. Concatenation and a learned projection fuse attended experience into DUET inputs. An LLM also rewrites styled instructions to Basic style, retaining the original below a confidence threshold. This rewriting occurs during navigation training; the learned fusion is not an entirely training-free method. Library capacity is tuned in the 50–100 range. The main paper delegates exact prompt, retrieval, confidence and training details to supplementary material.',novelty='Adds an LLM-derived experience library and explicit style conversion to GR-DUET-like persistent-scene navigation. GSA-VLN already tested text style translation; this paper’s contribution is the combined experience-to-policy interaction, not the first use of rewriting in this setting.',results='Table 1 Basic residential SR 69.3→70.8 versus GR-DUET; non-residential 56.6→58.4 (1.8 points, despite prose saying 2.2). Table 3 Scene SR/SPL 48.1/42.8→50.7/46.6. Table 2 Moira SPL drops 56.2→55.4 despite SR gain. Table 4 ablation rows differ from main full-model values; experimental configuration differences are not fully specified in the main text. Table 5 prints residential K=200 SR 63.1 and SPL 64.0, which is inconsistent with standard SPL≤SR and needs visual/source verification before using those numbers. Figure 2 logs Success, navigation error and nDTW, raising an unresolved question about whether evaluation-time reflection has privileged feedback. The case study calls a 1.5 m endpoint error a failure despite a 3 m SR threshold; it should be treated as illustrative, not independent benchmark evidence. Main results suggest modest gains but do not establish causal generalization from truthful experiences or compute efficiency including the VLM.',implementation='Official repository and CVF supplementary discovery pending. The main paper does not establish which logged success/trajectory metrics are available during test-time reflection. No code execution or reproducibility claim.',bottlenecks=[b('slow4fast-feedback','Experience quality and evaluation feedback access are insufficiently specified for an autonomous generalization claim.','Reflection on failed routes or noisy captions in unseen scenes, especially when success/nDTW are unavailable to a deployed agent.','LLM summaries may encode unsupported spatial rules; logged metric provenance and retrieval/confidence calibration need implementation verification.','METHOD: Figure 2 and §§2.3–2.6; uncertainty is an audit limitation rather than proven leakage.',['GSA-VLN','ATENA','FeedTTA'],'Uncertain because evidence is insufficient','Insufficiently tested', 'Medium')],sources=[dict(url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.09111',locator='Author manuscript and title')],limits=['Some extracted Table 5 cells and the incomplete retrieval paragraph require source/PDF verification. Supplement/repository audit pending.'])
render()
